The Saturn tells of its video, colour and sound memory.

Patch 0006 told the frontend where the two work RAMs are. A game's
sprites, backgrounds, colours and sound program live in four other
memories, and a RAM cartridge is a fifth; RAM Watch and RAM Search could
not reach any of them.

So each says where it is (MDFN_ChimeraWordMemory): the sound processor's
RAM at 0x05A00000, the sprite processor's (VDP1) memory at 0x05C00000, the
background processor's (VDP2) memory at 0x05E00000, the colour memory at
0x05F00000, and a RAM cartridge's two halves at 0x02400000 and 0x02600000.

Two of them cannot simply be written in place. The part of Mednafen that
draws the backgrounds keeps its own copy of the VDP2's memory and of the
colours, so a byte written only to the first copy would read back and
never be seen. And where a colour is kept depends on the colour mode. For
these the hook takes two functions, to read and to write a byte as the
console's processor would: the VDP2's memory is written through Mednafen's
own PokeVRAM, the colour memory through the functions a game's reads and
writes go through.

--- a/src/mednafen.h
+++ b/src/mednafen.h
@@ -34,7 +34,11 @@
 // A memory of the machine that is kept as 16-bit words in the host's byte
 // order, with the address the machine's processor finds it at. (Memories kept
 // as bytes are told through MDFNMP_AddRAM, as they always were.)
-void MDFN_ChimeraWordMemory(const char* name, uint32 address, uint16* words, uint32 bytes);
+// "peek" and "poke" are for a memory that cannot simply be read or written
+// in place: they take a byte's offset into the memory and do what the
+// machine's processor would. Without them the words are read and written
+// where they are.
+void MDFN_ChimeraWordMemory(const char* name, uint32 address, uint16* words, uint32 bytes, uint8 (*peek)(uint32 offset) = nullptr, void (*poke)(uint32 offset, uint8 value) = nullptr);
 // A memory the machine keeps while it is off (a battery-backed RAM), under
 // the extension Mednafen saves it with ("bkr"). "words" as above; a file of
 // such a memory holds the words high byte first.
